   ],
   "l": "Highest good is like water.\nWater is good at benefiting the ten-thousand things and yet does not contend,\nand dwells in what the multitude hate —",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The highest good is like water. Water is good at benefiting all things and never contends; it settles in the places people despise —",
   "n": "善 changes job between the two lines. First it is a noun, 'the good' (上善, 'the highest good'); then a verb, 'is good at' (善利, 'good at benefiting'). It will take a third job in the last unit of this chapter. 所 makes a noun out of the verb that follows it — 所惡 'what is hated' — and 之 hangs the doer on it: 眾人之所惡, 'what the multitude hate'. This is how the language says 'that which', and you will meet it constantly. And watch the reading of 惡: here it is wù, 'to hate'; in chapter 2 the same character was è, 'ugly'. Nothing on the page tells you which — only the sense does.",
   "p": true
  },
