   "l": "Therefore: close to the Way.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "And so it comes near to the Way.",
   "n": "No subject and no verb 'to be': the line simply says 'therefore near to the Way', and you carry water over from the sentence before. Wang Bi's comment fastens on that 'near' — the Way is without being, water is a thing that is, so water comes close to the Way without being it. 於 is the all-purpose preposition; here it marks what the nearness is to."
