   "n": "Seven lines of three characters, all the same shape: a topic, then 善, then what it excels in — 'as for dwelling, good in the ground'. Nothing marks the topic; position alone does the work, and this bare topic-and-comment is the commonest sentence in the book. 善 has now done three different jobs in one chapter: a noun in 上善 ('the highest good'); 'is good at' standing in front of another verb in 善利 ('good at benefiting'); and here the predicate itself, with what follows as its complement. 夫唯 X 故 Y is a fixed frame, 'it is precisely because X that Y' — and 夫 opening a sentence is read fú, not fū 'a man'. Many editions print 政 'government' where this one prints 正; the two characters stand in for each other constantly in early texts. The lines are traditionally taken as rhyming in two groups: 淵, 仁, 信 in one Old Chinese rhyme group, and 治, 能, 時 in another. In Mandarin (yuān, rén, xìn / zhì, néng, shí) only a faint echo of the first is left."
